<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orbelian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orbelians were an Armenian princely family who ruled much of Syunik and Vayots Dzor from the 13th to the 15th century. Branch of a family that had served the Georgian crown, they settled in Syunik under the Zakarid princes and later served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. Their rule saw a cultural revival, and the historian Stepanos Orbelian wrote a history of Syunik.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orbelian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Orbelian dynasty" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princes_Bughta_and_Burtel_Orbelians.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family took its name from the fortress of Orbeti in the south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, one of the lands it received with the hereditary title of amirspasalar, commander-in-chief, for its service at the Georgian court. In 1177 Ivane Orbeli led a rebellion against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> on behalf of the king's nephew, Prince Demna. George split the rebels, winning over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakarids">Zakarids</a> among others, and had Ivane killed; many Orbelians died with him, Demna was blinded and castrated, and the surviving Orbelians were expelled from Georgia and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Through the mediation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivane-mkhargrdzeli">Ivane Zakarian</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iv-of-georgia">George IV Lasha</a> appointed Liparit III Orbelian viceroy and granted him wide possessions in Syunik, with Gegharkunik and Vayots Dzor, and in Kotayk. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a> invaded in 1236, Elikum Orbelian kept his lands by accepting the authority of the Khan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> and sending troops on Mongol campaigns. Smbat II strengthened the family through his ties with the great Khan and stayed out of the Georgian revolts of 1249 and of 1259 to 1261; for his loyalty he received the title of king, and he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in 1273.</p><p>His younger brother Tarsaich ruled until 1290. Disputes among Tarsaich's heirs followed, and his eldest son Elikum, who ruled from 1290 to 1300, divided the family's possessions among his brothers, weakening the dynasty; his successor Burtel founded a collateral branch, the Burtelians. The principality declined with the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Tamerlane</a> in 1385: Prince Smbat Orbelian, besieged in the fortress of Vorotnaberd, was taken with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and returned only after a forced conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Smbat's grandson Rustam Orbelian became a counsellor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, but after the defeat of the Tabriz troops at Sofyan in 1437 he left Syunik for Lori and sold his lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatev-monastery">Tatev Monastery</a>. The family lost its influence and split into small branches, though many melik families of Syunik in the 16th to 19th centuries, among them the Orbelian meliks of Tatev, traced their descent to it. Its history is documented in inscriptions across Vayots Dzor and Syunik and in Bishop Stepanos's History of Syunik of 1297.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orbelian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local princely families such as the <b>Orbelians</b> later restored Armenian rule in Syunik under Georgian and Mongol overlords.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-syunik">Kingdom of Syunik</a></li><li><blockquote>They ruled through Armenian nobles such as the <b>Orbelians</b>, Vachutians and Hasan-Jalalyans, and trade on routes through Ani and Dvin revived.</blockquote><a href="/state/zakarid-armenia">Zakarid Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
